  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 239 AM EDT Thu Sep 17 2026\n\nAn approximate 600 dam upper high centered nearly overhead earlier, \nhas begun it's journey of weakening and cycling back west today. \nRecord heat for mid September will continue though. At the surface, \nhigh pressure over the E and NE, will also weaken, leaving our area \nmore susceptible to just enough instability for diurnal shower \ndevelopment. Isolated to scattered showers/storm will be possible \nfor the Southern Appalachians, NE Tennessee, and SW Virginia this \nafternoon and evening. Tomorrow may introduce some isolated to \nscattered development for parts of the central and northern valley. \nSummer-like precipitation chances for the afternoon and evening will \npersist into next week with possible expansion in coverage some days.\n\nLower to mid with some upper 90s, are forecast through the weekend, \nbefore a slight turn downward towards the end of the forecast period \nor as we approach the middle of next week. Upper 90s to low 100s \nheat indices will be possible from the southern valley up through \nthe central valley. The highest heat indices may peak tomorrow. \nThough most of the area won't reach Heat Advisory criteria, that \ndoesn't mean to let your guard down when it comes to being outdoors, \nstaying cool, and hydrated. Our daytime highs yesterday (before \nclimate officially runs after this discussion), tied the daily \nrecord at Tri-Cities. That makes Chattanooga's streak of 90 and \nabove at 44 days and counting, with no break in sight.",
